# ── Build artefacts ──────────────────────────────────────────
target/
dist/
# `cargo mutants` writes a log per mutant here; it is a run report, not source.
# `.old` is the same thing from a rename rather than a fresh run.
mutants.out/
mutants.out.old/

# ── Dioxus / dx CLI outputs ──────────────────────────────────
apps/*/dist/
apps/*/.dioxus/

# ── Downloaded assets (Ketcher, etc) ─────────────────────────
**/public/assets/ketcher/

# ── macOS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.AppleDouble
.LSOverride

# ── IDE / editor ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `.vscode/` is TRACKED, deliberately: `settings.json` there points rust-analyzer
# at nextest and at the shared target directory, and `extensions.json` is the
# list an editor needs to match the gate. Both are part of the toolchain config
# rather than personal preference, so they are committed rather than ignored.
#
# The negation is needed because a developer's global `core.excludesFile`
# commonly lists `.vscode/`. A `.gitignore` in the repository takes precedence
# over the global one, so `!.vscode/` is what actually un-ignores it; without
# the line, `git add .vscode/settings.json` fails on such a machine and succeeds
# on another, which is the worse of the two outcomes.
!.vscode/
.vscode/*
!.vscode/settings.json
!.vscode/extensions.json

# `.idea/` is the JetBrains one and is still ignored: the files in it are per
# machine (window layout, index state) and a shared one is noise.
.idea/
*.iml
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.DS_Store

# ── Environment / secrets ───────────────────────────────────
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local

# ── Pre-commit cache ────────────────────────────────────────
.pre-commit-cache/
# Coverage output. `cargo make cov` writes to `target/llvm-cov-report/`; this is
# the older `cargo tarpaulin` path and the `lcov.info` that lands beside it.
lcov.info
lcov.info.*
*.tarpaulin

SESSION_LOG.md

# ── LIPID MAPS data and the outputs of evolving it ──────────────────────────
# Everything below is produced by `smarts-evoliposuction`
# (`apps/lipid-selecto-rs/lipidmaps/smarts-evoliposuction`), and none of it is
# source. It is listed individually rather than as one pattern because the two
# largest files are third-party licensed data — `git add -A` on a machine that
# has run the tool once would otherwise put 55 MB of LIPID MAPS into history, and
# a licence is not something a later commit can take back.
#
#   LMSD.sdf.zip     21 MB, downloaded from lipidmaps.org by
#                    `smarts-evoliposuction download`. Third-party data.
#   LMSD.sdf.tsv     33 MB, the same records as the zip, converted to TSV.
#   example_lipids.smi  the scratch input used while developing a rule set.
#   smarts_results.csv  the tool's default output name, so it lands in whatever
#                    directory the tool was run from — including this one.
#   smiles_sets/,     70 MB of SMILES split out of the TSV, and the tested
#   smarts_results_    SMARTS per class.
#   tested_smarts/
#
# A SMARTS rule set *is* worth committing — it is a result, reproducible from a
# seed — but it belongs in the rule library, not as whatever the tool last
# happened to write. Nothing here is that.
#
# `.gitignore` is not a lock: `git add -f` overrides it, and a file already
# tracked stays tracked however many rules are added here. The
# `check-added-large-files` pre-commit hook (`--maxkb=2048`) is the backstop for
# the two large ones; the small ones are caught by
# `crates/upload/tests/gate_consistency.rs`, which fails if a rule below is
# deleted, so this list cannot quietly stop working.
#
# The tool's own README.md and src/ *are* tracked — only its data is not.
/apps/lipid-selecto-rs/lipidmaps/smarts-evoliposuction/LMSD.sdf.zip
/apps/lipid-selecto-rs/lipidmaps/smarts-evoliposuction/LMSD.sdf.tsv
/apps/lipid-selecto-rs/lipidmaps/smarts-evoliposuction/example_lipids.smi
/smarts_results.csv
/example_lipids.smi
/smarts_results_tested_smarts/
/smiles_sets/

# Knowledge graph outputs
graphify-out/

.mcp.json
AGENTS.md
opencode.json
